      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "A 59-year-old man developed postural headache associated with a low CSF pressure. A CT scan revealed no abnormal findings and the orthostatic symptoms resolved without treatment 6 weeks after onset. He was diagnosed as having spontaneous intracranial hypotension (SIH) and remained symptom-free until he experienced recurrence of postural headache 9 months later. A lumbar puncture demonstrated low CSF pressure, and a CT scan revealed slit-like ventricles with narrowing of the sulci, Sylvian fissures, and infratentorial cisterns, in addition to bilateral subdural masses. After draining the hematomas, his symptoms resolved completely, and a follow-up CT scan was normal. We hypothesize that recurrent SIH in this case was due to small recurrent tears of a root sleeve. This case emphasizes the importance of follow-up of SIH for at least 9 months after resolution of symptoms."},
